<header><p class="st-kicker">colony · Horn and East Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">French Territory of the Afars and the Issas</h1><p class="st-meta">1967 CE – 1977 CE · capital Djibouti</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The last name of France's colony in Djibouti, adopted in 1967 to stress its Afar and Issa peoples, before independence in 1977.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After a referendum in March 1967, in which a majority voted to remain French amid protests and allegations of fraud, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/french-somaliland">French Somaliland</a> was renamed the French Territory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afar">Afars</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/issa-clan">Issas</a>. The name aimed to emphasise the Afar community, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> favoured over the Issa <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somalis</a>, who were suspected of favouring union with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/somalia">Somalia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> governed through a high commissioner and a local government headed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afar">Afar</a> politician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ali-aref-bourhan">Ali Aref Bourhan</a>. Restrictions on residence in the capital, enforced with a barrier around <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/djibouti">Djibouti</a> city, targeted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somalis">Somali</a> migrants. The port and the railway to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/federal-democratic-republic-of-ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> remained the economic base, and the French military kept a large garrison. Nationalists such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hassan-gouled-aptidon">Hassan Gouled Aptidon</a> pressed for independence, and in 1976 a hostage crisis at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loyada-hostage-crisis">Loyada</a> drew world attention.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In May 1977 voters overwhelmingly approved independence, and on 27 June 1977 the territory became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/djibouti">Republic of Djibouti</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hassan-gouled-aptidon">Hassan Gouled Aptidon</a> as president.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/french-territory-of-the-afars-and-the-issas.jpg" alt="French Territory of the Afars and the Issas" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Original: Unknown Vector: SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_France.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1967 CE – 1977 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>colony</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>colonial</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Djibouti</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ali Aref Bourhan</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>French, Afar, Somali</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/djibouti">Djibouti</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Territoire francais des Afars et des Issas, TFAI</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1972&amp;focus=french-territory-of-the-afars-and-the-issas" aria-label="French Territory of the Afars and the Issas on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5867 1745 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where French Territory of the Afars and the Issas lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6187 2001L6187 2001M6187 2001L5054 728M6187 2001L6187 2001"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6187 2001h0M5054 728h0M6187 2001h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6187 2001h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6187 2001h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Djibouti. <a href="/atlas?year=1972&amp;focus=french-territory-of-the-afars-and-the-issas">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
